Let $n$ be the number of cells in a primitive dinosaur, and let $S$ be the set of cells of the dinosaur. We want to find the maximum $n$ such that $S$ cannot be partitioned into two or more subsets $S_1, S_2, \dots, S_k$ ($k \ge 2$) where each $S_i$ is a connected figure with $|S_i| \ge 2007$. A known result for this problem is that a primitive dinosaur can have at most $2(2007) - 2 = 4012$ cells.
If a dinosaur has $n \ge 4013$ cells, we can show it is not primitive by considering a spanning tree of the dual graph of the cells and removing an edge to partition it into two connected components, each of which must contain at least 2007 cells by the properties of tree partitioning, thus the maximum number of cells is $2(2007) - 2 = 4012$.

\boxed{4012}

A knight always moves from a white square to a black square, so no two knights attack each other if all knights are placed on squares of the same color; since there are 32 white squares and 32 black squares on an $8 \times 8$ board, the number of ways to place 31 knights on squares of one color is $\binom{32}{31} + \binom{32}{31} = 32 + 32 = 64$.
Since the graph of the knight's moves on an $8 \times 8$ board is bipartite, the maximum independent set of the board is 32, and it has been proven that for $n=31$, any independent set of size 31 must be a subset of either the set of all white squares or the set of all black squares, so the total number of ways is indeed 64.

\boxed{64}

