The labyrinth $\mathfrak{L}$ divides the plane into a set of regions, which can be viewed as the vertices and faces of a planar graph where the intersections of the lines are vertices and the segments of the lines are edges; by assigning a sign $s_i \in \{+1, -1\}$ to each side of line $L_i$, we define a region by the tuple of signs $(s_1, \dots, s_n)$, and two regions are connected by a door if and only if they differ by exactly one sign, which implies that the labyrinth is equivalent to the graph of an $n$-dimensional hypercube where the vertices are the regions and the edges represent the doors.
The graph of regions is isomorphic to the graph of an $n$-dimensional hypercube, where each vertex represents a region and two vertices are adjacent if the corresponding regions are separated by a door; the problem of finding the maximum number of knights who cannot reach each other is equivalent to finding the size of the maximum independent set of this graph, which is known to be $2^{n-1}$.

\boxed{2^{n-1}}

