Let $f(x) = \sqrt[N]{\frac{x^{2N}+1}{2}} - x$. We want to find the smallest $a_n$ such that $f(x) \le a_n(x-1)^2$ for all $x$. Since $f(1) = \sqrt[N]{1} - 1 = 0$, $a_n$ must satisfy $a_n \ge \frac{f''(1)}{2}$ by Taylor expansion around $x=1$. Calculating the derivatives, $f'(x) = \frac{1}{N} \left( \frac{x^{2N}+1}{2} \right)^{\frac{1}{N}-1} (x^{2N-1}) - 1$, so $f'(1) = \frac{1}{N} (1)^{1-N} (1) - 1 = \frac{1}{N} - 1$.
$f''(x) = \frac{1}{N} \left[ \left( \frac{1}{N} - 1 \right) \left( \frac{x^{2N}+1}{2} \right)^{\frac{1}{N}-2} (x^{2N-1})^2 + \left( \frac{x^{2N}+1}{2} \right)^{\frac{1}{N}-1} (2N-1)x^{2N-2} \right]$, so $f''(1) = \frac{1}{N} \left[ \left( \frac{1}{N} - 1 \right) + (2N-1) \right] = \frac{1}{N} (2N - \frac{1}{N}) = 2 - \frac{1}{N^2}$.
The value $a_n = \frac{f''(1)}{2} = \frac{2 - 1/N^2}{2} = 1 - \frac{1}{2N^2}$ is the candidate for the smallest real number, which leads to \boxed{1-\frac{1}{2 \cdot 2^{2n}}}

